course: dictionary
lesson: 1987
title: Compression: squashing and squeezing
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Compression is pressing or squeezing something so it takes up less space. The context gives you the clue: «Compression is a force that squashes; tension is a force that stretches.» So compression is the squashing push, and tension is the pulling apart.
explain: Compression is also a word in computing and first aid. In computing, file compression makes a file smaller so it is quicker to send. In first aid, chest compressions are pushes on the chest during CPR, which help keep blood moving when the heart stops.
explain: Use the words around it to choose the right meaning. «Compression squashes a part, like the legs of a table under a heavy load.» The legs are being squashed, so here compression means being pressed together. Ask yourself: is something being squashed, made smaller, or pushed on?
fact: Compression means squashing or squeezing something so it takes up less space.
fact: «Steel is strong in both compression and tension.»
fact: «Stone, brick, concrete and glass are strong in compression but weak in tension.»
fact: In science, gases can be compressed, but solids hardly at all.
fact: In computing, compression makes photos and videos smaller so they are quicker to send.
exercise: «Compression squashes a part, like the legs of a table under a heavy load.» What does compression do to the legs? | It makes the table lighter | It stretches them apart | *It squashes them together | It paints them a new colour
exercise: In a computer, what does file compression do? | *It makes the file smaller so it is quicker to send | It makes the file louder | It deletes the file for ever | It stretches the file to make it longer
exercise: «Stone, brick, concrete and glass are strong in compression but weak in tension.» Which would most likely break a brick? | Being squashed hard from above | Being painted red | *Being pulled apart from both ends | Being kept dry in a shed
test: In science, which can be compressed most easily? | *A gas | A solid rock | A steel bar | A glass window
test: In first aid, what are chest compressions? | Long, slow breaths into the lungs | Gentle taps on the back | *Pushes on the chest during CPR, to help keep blood moving | Stretches of the arms to keep warm
exam: Compression is a force that squashes. Which force stretches things apart? | Gravity | Friction | Magnetism | *Tension
